// Example 2: The Cave
// A simple adventure demonstrating branching narrative

=== start ===

You stand at the entrance of a dark cave.

The air is cool and damp. You can hear water dripping somewhere deep inside.

* [Enter the cave] -> inside
* [Walk away] -> walk_away

=== inside ===

You step into the darkness. Your eyes slowly adjust.

The cave splits into two passages.

* [Take the left passage] -> left_passage
* [Take the right passage] -> right_passage
* [Go back outside] -> start

=== left_passage ===

You walk down the left passage. It grows narrower and narrower.

Eventually, you reach a dead end. There's something glittering in the wall...

It's a vein of gold! But you have no tools to mine it.

* [Go back] -> inside

=== right_passage ===

The right passage opens into a large cavern.

In the center, you see a chest. It's unlocked.

* [Open the chest] -> treasure
* [Leave it alone] -> leave_chest

=== treasure ===

You open the chest and find it filled with ancient coins!

You're rich! You grab what you can carry and head back.

-> victory

=== leave_chest ===

You decide not to disturb the chest. Who knows what traps might be waiting?

You carefully back away and return to the entrance.

-> careful_end

=== walk_away ===

You decide the cave is too dangerous.

Sometimes discretion is the better part of valor.

-> careful_end

=== victory ===

THE END

You found treasure and returned safely!

=== careful_end ===

THE END

You survived, but wonder what you might have missed.
